{"author":{"id":"c7109691837125ce179ae67c00dda2acb755e6dfb3646ed3e9aad234d639bbcc"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786002417,"nonce":"5/E+LrUYG3MiOvbZjhYFOin3z+g=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDORPa0Bs8AAAABLrni8Q","github-url":"https://github.com/FhG-IISB/jNO/issues/95","origin":"github"},"title":"`.sized()` is silently ignored on compound shapes, so per-region mesh refinement never reaches gmsh","message":"## What happened\n\n`Shape.sized(h)` sets the target element size on the node it's called on. On a **compound** shape — anything from a CSG op (`|`, `-`, `\u0026`) — the size is stored but never read, so the mesher never sees it. Only single primitives get their size honoured. No error, no warning: the mesh is just built at the background size.\n## Minimal reproducer\n\n```python\nimport jno\n\nbox = jno.Shape.box\npart = box(0., 0., 0., 1., 1., 1.) | box(1., 0., 0., 2., 1., 1.)  # compound: two boxes fused\nbg   = box(-1., -1., -1., 3., 2., 2.)\n\nfor h in (1.0, 0.5, 0.2):\n    d = (part.name(\"part\").sized(h) + bg.name(\"bg\").sized(1.0)).domain()\n    print(f\"requested part h={h}: {len(d.mesh.cells_dict['tetra'])} tets\")\n```\n\n## Expected vs actual\n\nExpected — the mesh refines as `h` shrinks:\n\n```text\nrequested part h=1.0:    358 tets\nrequested part h=0.5:   1641 tets\nrequested part h=0.2:  12382 tets\n```\n\nActual — `sized()` has no effect at all; identical mesh every time:\n\n```text\nrequested part h=1.0:    358 tets\nrequested part h=0.5:    358 tets\nrequested part h=0.2:    358 tets\n```\n\nReplacing `part` with a single `box(0., 0., 0., 2., 1., 1.)` makes it work, which isolates the trigger to the region being a compound rather than a primitive.\n\n\n## Additional context\n\n**Root cause.** `Shape.leaves()` only reads a size at leaf nodes; walking a boolean node returns the children's sizes (all `None`) and drops the compound's own `_size`:\n\n```python\nif kind == \"leaf\":\n    return ((prim, self._size, key),)          # size read here only\nif kind in (\"cut\", \"fuse\", \"inter\"):\n    return node[1].leaves() + node[2].leaves() # self._size dropped\n```\n\n`emit._apply_size_fields` builds its gmsh Distance/Threshold fields from `leaves()`, so compound sizes never become size fields. It also takes the background from `max(...)` over the sizes it found — so if the only survivor is a coarse region, `SizeMin == SizeMax` and the field degenerates to uniform, removing grading everywhere.\n\n**Impact.** In a 3-D eddy-current model (ELP 32/6/20 planar transformer), the windings are `outer - inner` washers, i.e. compounds. Requested 0.1 mm, realised 1.43 mm — one sliver through each 0.45 mm conductor against a 0.66 mm skin depth. The model solved cleanly at ~40k DOF and gave a plausible loss, so the under-resolution stayed invisible until a convergence study was attempted.","files":null}]}